  "id": "Compound::DB00761 - Gene::6558",
  "verbalization": "Potassium Chloride is shown to interact with multiple members of the Slc12 family, specifically Slc12a5, Slc12a6, and Slc12a7, which are all involved in cation-coupled chloride cotransporters. These transporters are central to the movement of chloride ions across cell membranes in response to changes in intracellular potassium levels. The evidence suggests that Potassium Chloride may influence the activity of these transporters, potentially modulating chloride transport in cells. The high scores from the three paths indicate a consistent and significant interaction between Potassium Chloride and Slc12a2, which is a key component of the cation-coupled chloride cotransport system. The shared involvement of these transporters in the same biological process supports the idea that Potassium Chloride may regulate the function of Slc12a2 through these pathways. The relationship is supported by the presence of multiple binding and functional interactions, though the exact mechanism of regulation remains to be fully elucidated.",
